2 a) Operation	                         System Call
file creation/open	                     open() (with O_CREAT flag) or creat()
write to file                            write()
read from file                           read()
close file	                             close()

b)  open()
i)opens an existing file or creates a new file (using the O_CREAT flag).
ii)returns a file descriptor that is used for subsequent file operations.
    write()
i)writes data from a buffer into a file.
ii)returns the number of bytes successfully written.

c)#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>
int main() 
{
    int fd;
    fd = open("student.txt", O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, 0644);
    write(fd, "susi\n", strlen("susi\n"));
    close(fd);
    return 0;
}


3 a) The required system calls are:
open() – open the source and destination files.
read() – read data from the source file.
write() – write data to the destination file.
close() – close both files after copying.

b) read()
i)reads data from the source file into a buffer.
ii)returns the number of bytes read.
  write()
i)writes the data from the buffer into the destination file.
ii)returns the number of bytes written.

c) #include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
int main()
{
 int src, dest;
 char buffer[100];
 int bytesRead;
 src = open("source.txt", O_RDONLY)
 dest = open("destination.txt", O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, 0644);
 while ((bytesRead = read(src, buffer, sizeof(buffer))) > 0)
{
    write(dest, buffer, bytesRead);
}
    close(src);
    close(dest);
    return 0;
}


4 a) fork()
i)creates a new child process by duplicating the parent process.
ii)returns 0 in the child process and the child's Process ID (PID) in the parent process.
     exec()
i)replaces the current process with a new program.
ii)the process ID (PID) remains the same, but the program being executed changes.

b)the wait() system call makes the parent process wait until the child process finishes execution.
it allows the parent to collect the child's exit status.

c)#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
int main()
{
    pid_t pid;
    pid = fork();   
    if (pid == 0)
{
     execl("/bin/ls", "ls", NULL);
} 
    else if (pid > 0) 
{
     wait(NULL);
     printf("Child process completed.\n");
}
    else
{
    printf("Fork failed!\n");
}
 return 0;
}


5 a)File management is an important function of an operating system because it:
*organizes and stores data efficiently on secondary storage.
*allows users to create, read, write, rename, and delete files.
*protects files from unauthorized access using permissions.
*ensures efficient retrieval and management of stored data.

b) open()
i)opens an existing file or creates a new file (with appropriate flags).
ii)returns a file descriptor used for file operations.
  close()
i)closes an opened file.
ii)releases the file descriptor and frees system resources.

c)#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
int main() 
{
    int fd;
    char buffer[100];
    int bytesRead;
    fd = open("student.txt", O_RDONLY);
    bytesRead = read(fd, buffer, sizeof(buffer) - 1);
    buffer[bytesRead] = '\0';
    printf("%s", buffer);
    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
